<section suppressAutoName="true" suppressAutoNumber="true"><title>Difference form definition</title>
 
<p>A discrete dynamical system is in <term>difference form</term> when it is written so that the left hand side gives the difference in the state variable over one time step.</p>

<p>If the state variable is <m>x_n</m>, then a dynamical system is in <term>difference form</term> when it is of the form
<me>x_{n+1}−x_n=f(x_n).</me></p>

<p>For example, the dynamical system
<me>x_{n+1}−x_n=3x_n</me>
is written in <term>difference form</term> with <m>f(x)=3x.</m></p>

<p>An alternative form is <term><ref to="doenet:82YaRL8CieueYqLgxMpxye">function iteration form</ref></term>, such as
<me>x_{n+1}=4x_n.</me>
</p>

<p>This dynamical system is identical to the previous one, as one can see by subtracting <m>x_n</m> from both sides.</p>
</section>